ADS-009 — Canonical Trust Object (CTO)

Document ID: ADS-009
Title: Canonical Trust Object (CTO)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Trust Object (CTO), the standardized representation of trust assertions within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

A Canonical Trust Object captures identity, authentication, authorization, assurance, security classification, and trust relationships in a governed, traceable, and interoperable form.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Trust model
Identity assertions
Authentication context
Authorization context
Trust relationships
Assurance levels
Trust lifecycle
Trust governance

This specification applies to every trust assertion within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Trust Object (CTO) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object that represents a verifiable trust assertion about an identity, participant, object, interaction, or decision within the Atlas ecosystem.

A CTO expresses why an entity can be trusted under defined conditions.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Trust Object SHALL:

Represent trust assertions.
Support identity verification.
Support authentication.
Support authorization.
Support security governance.
Support auditing.
Support interoperability.
Preserve trust provenance.
5. Inheritance

The Canonical Trust Object SHALL inherit every mandatory property of the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
 Canonical Trust Object
        │
 ┌──────┼──────────────┐
 ▼      ▼              ▼
Identity Authentication Authorization
Trust    Trust          Trust

Future trust profiles SHALL extend this hierarchy.

6. Trust Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical trust categories.

Category	Description
Identity Trust	Verification of participant identity
Authentication Trust	Verification of credentials
Authorization Trust	Permission to perform actions
Communication Trust	Trust in message exchange
Artifact Trust	Trust in engineering artifacts
Decision Trust	Trust in engineering decisions
Organization Trust	Trust relationships between organizations
Platform Trust	Trust in platform components
7. Mandatory Trust Properties

Every CTO SHALL define:

Property	Description
Trust ID	Globally unique identifier
Trust Type	Canonical trust classification
Subject	Entity being evaluated
Issuer	Authority making the assertion
Scope	Boundaries of the assertion
Assurance Level	Confidence in the assertion
Valid From	Start of validity
Valid Until	End of validity
Trust Status	Current operational state
8. Identity Model

Each CTO SHALL identify:

Subject identity
Identity type
Organization
Role(s)
Attributes
Credential references
Identity status

Identity SHALL remain uniquely identifiable throughout the trust lifecycle.

9. Authentication Model

Authentication assertions SHALL include:

Authentication method
Authentication timestamp
Authentication authority
Credential type
Credential status
Authentication outcome

Authentication SHALL be independently verifiable.

10. Authorization Model

Authorization assertions SHALL define:

Authorized operations
Applicable policies
Resource scope
Constraints
Delegations
Expiration conditions

Authorization SHALL be policy-driven.

11. Assurance Levels

Atlas defines the following canonical assurance levels.

Level	Description
AL0	Unverified
AL1	Basic verification
AL2	Verified identity
AL3	Strong assurance
AL4	High assurance
AL5	Mission-critical assurance

Organizations MAY define additional profiles while preserving these canonical semantics.

12. Trust Relationships

Trust relationships SHALL be explicit.

Identity
     │
authenticatedBy
     ▼
Authentication
     │
authorizedBy
     ▼
Authorization
     │
governedBy
     ▼
Policy

Trust chains SHALL remain traceable.

13. Trust Lifecycle

Every CTO SHALL support the following lifecycle.

Issued
   │
Validated
   │
Active
   │
Suspended
   │
Revoked
   │
Archived

Lifecycle transitions SHALL be auditable.

14. Provenance

Every CTO SHALL preserve:

Issuing authority
Issuance method
Supporting evidence
Verification history
Applicable policies
Trust chain
Validation history

Trust provenance SHALL be immutable.

15. Quality Requirements

Every CTO SHALL satisfy:

Authenticity
Integrity
Traceability
Verifiability
Non-repudiation
Policy compliance
Security consistency
Governance compliance
16. Governance

Every CTO SHALL define:

Owner
Issuing authority
Validation authority
Revocation authority
Security policy
Retention policy
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain attached to the trust object throughout its lifecycle.

17. Conformance

A Canonical Trust Object conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Implements mandatory trust properties.
Supports identity assertions.
Supports authentication and authorization.
Preserves trust provenance.
Supports lifecycle management.
Implements assurance levels.
18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Federated trust ecosystems.
Cross-organization trust delegation.
Decentralized identity integration.
Verifiable credentials.
Post-quantum trust mechanisms.
Adaptive assurance models.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical trust semantics.

